Subject: wasix: poll a socket's descriptor on the thread that called
 poll_oneoff

A guest waiting in poll_oneoff on a socket paid the same handoff through
the selector thread an epoll wait did, and one thing more: the guard
that waits for a socket registered it with the selector, permanently and
edge triggered, and allocated itself on every call.

A read or write subscription on a socket with a host descriptor is now
polled by the calling thread through `try_suspend_on_pollfd`, in
`direct_poll_oneoff`. It gets no guard, so it is registered nowhere and
nothing reads it; clock subscriptions become the deadline; every other
subscription - a pipe, an event notification, a file, a socket the host
cannot name - gets its guard exactly as before and joins the same wait
through the thread's waker. A turn polls each guard once, then waits on
the descriptors; a wake from either side ends it and both are looked at
again. A turn whose guards produced events still probes the sockets with
a deadline that has passed before it answers, so a guard that stays
ready cannot hide a socket. What a wait reports is what the guest then
reads, and nothing in between consumes anything.

Nothing is claimed and nothing is kept between calls, because
poll_oneoff names its descriptors every time, so a call that cannot be
served this way needs no undoing: it falls through whole to the existing
path, which builds the guards. That happens for a journalled call, a
runtime with no descriptor wait, more sockets than one wait accepts
(`DIRECT_POLL_CAP`, 64), a signal that cannot be delivered, and a
descriptor that can no longer be named - though a call that has already
collected events from its guards answers with those, since a subset is a
legal answer. Every turn runs the pending dynamic link operations first
and asks whether the thread was finished after capturing its interrupt
generation, as the epoll loop does, and a call resumed from a deep sleep
answers with what its wait produced before anything else runs.

A readable descriptor reports one byte, not a count. Of the readers of a
poll_oneoff answer in wasix-libc, poll() and pselect() use the type and
hangup flag and never the count, and ioctl(FIONREAD) is what the guests
test for zero. A peer that has gone away or shut its writing half down
may have sent a last request first, so on that turn the wait says
whether bytes are still unread: with some, a read subscription is an
ordinary readable descriptor; with none it reports no bytes and the
hangup flag, as a guard reports the end of a file. An error reports a
code poll() turns into POLLERR. The half-close is asked for as a bit of
its own rather than folded into hangup, which would tell an epoll set
that a stream it can still write to has hung up.

